  "text": "A small shop's checkout works. Then the business asks for a second shipping rule, a text message beside the confirmation email, and a count for the loyalty program. Each request is small. Whether it lands as a new piece, or as an edit to code that already works, depends on how the objects were drawn. Fifteen questions on object-oriented design, argued on that one shop. Eight get a figure each, and seven go quickly at the end.\n\nQuestion one. What are cohesion and coupling? Cohesion is how focused a class is: its methods and data all serve one clear purpose. Coupling is how much modules depend on each other. Low coupling means a change in one rarely forces a change in another. Here's the anti-pattern. One order manager prices the cart, charges the card, writes the invoice, sends the email and updates the stock. It's a god class, a pocketknife with a blade for everything. That's five reasons to change in one class, and everything that touches an order depends on all five. A new email template means retesting checkout. Split it into five focused pieces, each with one job and a narrow connection to the rest: high cohesion, low coupling. Most of solid pushes this way. To spot one in a review, count a class's reasons to change, and count what has to change with it.",
